    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm having some trouble with a series of walnut cabinet builds. I'm cutting dovetail joints, but I'm getting consistent splintering at the very tips of the pins. It's messing up the fit and making the joinery look messy. Any advice on how to manage the grain or what I should check with my chisels to stop this happening?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "This splintering often happens when the chisel isn't perfectly aligned with the grain, especially at the end of the joint. Ensure your chisel is razor-sharp and that you are cutting with the grain, or at least very close to the line of the cut. Sometimes, using a very thin block of scrap wood as a sacrificial surface for your shallow paring passes can help control tear-out near the tips.",
      "source": "target",
