    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "tried that with a thin sacrificial piece, but it didn't really help much. the splintering is happening even when I'm being super careful with the grain direction. could it be the moisture content in the walnut? or maybe the angle of the bevel on my chisels is too blunt for these fine details?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It is quite possible that the moisture content of the walnut is contributing to the grain instability, especially if the wood has dried unevenly. Additionally, if your chisels have a dull or excessively rounded bevel, they can push fibers rather than cleanly slicing them away, leading to tear-out. Try slightly refining the bevel angle for superior cutting action, and always consider acclimating the walnut to your shop environment before cutting detailed joints.",
      "source": "target",
