    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'll check the acclimation, but the wood feels pretty stable to me. Honestly, it's almost like the wood is actively resisting the cut at those specific points, like something is just grabbing the fibers and pulling them out right before the blade hits. It feels more erratic than just a dull tool. Is there any chance the grain pattern itself is fundamentally unpredictable in this batch?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If the resistance feels erratic rather than just consistently tear-out, the grain structure itself might be showing unpredictable movement, which is common in figured walnut. Extremely tight or highly figured wood grain can sometimes behave unpredictably, making it difficult for any tool to manage uniformly. In these tricky spots, consider slightly adjusting your chisel angle inward to take a shallower, more patient cut, allowing the blade to \"slice\" the fibers cleanly rather than trying to cleave them.",
      "source": "target",
